{
  "dom_id": "step-q154633",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If $|7y+3|=1,$ then $y=$",
  "worked_solution": "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|7y+3|=7y+3,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|7y+3| & =1 \\\\ 7y+3 & =1 \\\\ 7y & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{7y}{7} & =\\frac{-2}{7} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|7y+3|=-(7y+3),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|7y+3| & =1 \\\\ -(7y+3) & =1 \\\\ -7y-3 & =1 \\\\ -7y & =4 \\\\ \\frac{-7y}{-7} & =\\frac{4}{-7} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{4}{7}$ and $y=-\\frac{2}{7}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
